WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.050
- We're about to watch
every "Harry Potter" movie,

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:04.640
well, scenes from "Harry Potter" movies,

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:05.660
and I'm gonna critique them

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.820
from the eyes and lens of a doctor.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.370
You ready? (energy zapping) Pyu!

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.978
Let us begin "The Sorcerer's
Stone". I just spit.

00:00:13.978 --> 00:00:16.870
(dramatic music)

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:17.832
Harry Potter.

00:00:17.832 --> 00:00:19.999
(dramatic music continues)

00:00:19.999 --> 00:00:20.832
(Hagrid sniffling)

00:00:20.832 --> 00:00:23.570
That's Hagrid, and that's Dumbledore.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:25.161
I remember their names.

00:00:25.161 --> 00:00:27.477
- Good luck, Harry Potter.

00:00:27.477 --> 00:00:29.420
- Oh, that's an interesting
scar. You know what's funny?

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.790
I've watched Harry Potter as a kid.

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:32.180
Never thought about this before.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.820
Went to medical school,
residency, and now I see it.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.820
I think Harry was treated
by a bad plastic surgeon

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.390
who was trying to practice Z-plasty.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.860
Z-plasty is what happens when you try

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.980
and make a scar look less visible.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.110
When you cut a scar in a shape of a Z,

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:53.130
you can actually change its appearance

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.390
to blend in more when it heals,

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.620
when it blends in better
with the skin lines.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:58.740
Isn't that cool?

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.206
- Neville, come down.
- You're not supposed

00:01:00.206 --> 00:01:01.306
to take off yet.
- Neville!

00:01:01.306 --> 00:01:02.540
(Neville exclaiming)

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.040
See, I feel like if you're riding a broom

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.390
for the first time, it should
be just like riding a bike,

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.920
you need to wear a helmet.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.290
Clearly, they don't have doctors,

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:13.693
family medicine doctors doing
counseling at this school.

00:01:13.693 --> 00:01:15.018
Ooh.
(Neville grunting)

00:01:15.018 --> 00:01:16.501
(broom whooshing)

00:01:16.501 --> 00:01:17.470
- [Neville] Ow!

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.020
- [Madame Hooch] Everyone out of the way!

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.000
- Okay, that's a high-velocity fall.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.970
I would say it's a fall
from probably 12 feet.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:26.140
That requires emergency 911 help.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:29.590
You need to stabilize the
C-spine, get a C-collar on there.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.440
If you have a fracture
in the cervical collar,

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.780
you could potentially cause paralysis.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.188
- Ow, ow, ow.
- Oh, oh, oh, oh.

00:01:35.188 --> 00:01:37.260
Oh dear, it's a broken wrist.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:38.710
- It's a broken wrist, that's it?

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:41.310
Maybe do a full body check
like when a patient comes in

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.650
from a trauma, we check the entire body.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.000
We just cut off all the clothes

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.410
because we need to see everything.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.830
The last thing we wanna find out

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.600
is that there's like a huge
cut from the broomstick

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.680
inside his abdomen.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.210
He's bleeding out, and we didn't check

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.120
'cause we're going,
"Oh, look at his wrist."

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.040
Most commonly broken bone in the wrist?

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.670
Scaphoid. You got it. Good job.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:00.450
Falling on an outstretched hand.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.620
I feel like I need a trauma specialist

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:03.530
watching this with me.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:05.379
I'm scared for all of their bodies.

00:02:05.379 --> 00:02:06.754
(Draco screaming)

00:02:06.754 --> 00:02:09.737
(intense music)

00:02:09.737 --> 00:02:11.104
Ooh.

00:02:11.104 --> 00:02:12.323
(Draco grunting)

00:02:12.323 --> 00:02:15.240
See, so that fall, while looking terrible

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.170
and at high velocity did
not look as bad to me

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:19.920
as a medical professional, I'll explain.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.780
Because he fell with
momentum carrying him forward

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:26.810
and inertia, he actually did
not come to a sudden stop

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:29.410
therefore the force and
the impact on his body

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:31.710
wasn't as bad, so that's
why a lot of times

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.460
like when people survive
sky diving accidents,

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:35.700
it's because they landed on a hill

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.238
and were able to continue falling down.

00:02:37.238 --> 00:02:39.820
(ball crashing)

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.240
What is that?

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.070
- Finite Incantatem! (energy blasting)

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.790
- That's what I say
when patients misbehave.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:47.660
- Are you okay?

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:50.010
- No, I think my, I think my arm's broken.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:53.540
- It's either a radial
fracture or an ulnar fracture

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:54.723
based on the location.

00:02:54.723 --> 00:02:56.223
- Brackium Emendo!

00:02:57.365 --> 00:03:00.115
(energy humming)

00:03:01.998 --> 00:03:03.370
(arm squelching)
- Ugh.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.170
- Oh, it looks like he
got rid of his bones.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:06.710
If you don't have any bones in there,

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:08.560
you can't really make use of the hand.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.170
And then you could potentially damage

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:11.950
all your blood vessels and nerves there.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:14.730
- I can mend bones in the
heartbeat but growing them back...

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.340
- You will be able to, won't you?

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.110
- Oh, I'll be able to
certainly, but it'll be painful.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.040
- Growing bones back?
That's something new.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.440
Maybe we'll have that in the future.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.470
There's actually a
condition fibular hemimelia

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.590
where the fibula of
the lower leg is short,

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:31.350
and we could actually do some
procedures to help it grow.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.839
Limb lengthening is actually
a legitimate procedure.

00:03:33.839 --> 00:03:35.647
(perilous music)

00:03:35.647 --> 00:03:38.330
(sword whooshing)

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:39.900
Dragon!

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.369
I've never experienced a
dragon bite in the hospital.

00:03:42.369 --> 00:03:43.613
- [Sam] Basilisk.

00:03:43.613 --> 00:03:45.140
- A basilisk dragon?

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.930
What's the difference between a basilisk

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:47.763
and a dragon?
- Couldn't have even tell you.

00:03:47.763 --> 00:03:50.740
- I have never seen a basilisk
injury in the hospital.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.880
There was someone who had
like a Komodo dragon bite,

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:54.932
which resulted in salmonella, I think.

00:03:54.932 --> 00:03:55.765
- [Sam] Wow.

00:03:55.765 --> 00:03:57.550
Yeah, I'm pretty sure
salmonella is the bacteria

00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:59.120
that's found on lizard
tongues though like,

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:01.133
or teeth that you need to be careful for.

00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:03.310
(sword clanking)

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:05.290
Oh, that's definitely one
way to kill a basilisk.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:07.511
Go right for the base
of the basilisk skull.

00:04:07.511 --> 00:04:09.169
(basilisk screaming)

00:04:09.169 --> 00:04:11.468
(Harry screaming)

00:04:11.468 --> 00:04:15.780
Ooh. A tooth to the forearm
radial ulnar artery.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.730
He better put some
pressure on that bad boy

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:20.550
or a tourniquet if it's bleeding badly

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:22.560
'cause he's not gonna
survive long otherwise.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.290
And there must be salmonella
on this basilisk tooth.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.087
Watch like another doctor
do a reaction video.

00:04:27.087 --> 00:04:29.060
"Dr. Mike shares misinformation.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:31.170
There's no salmonella on basilisk teeth.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.827
I've looked into it. I read
the research on Facebook."

00:04:34.827 --> 00:04:37.577
(dramatic music)

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:43.930
- [Harry] Of course, phoenix
tears have healing powers.

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:45.950
- So, all you gotta do to heal yourself

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:48.240
